WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:00.834 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Welcome back.

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
-As I live and breathe,
I'm back.

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:04.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And congratulations
on a Pulitzer!

00:00:04.304 --> 00:00:04.838 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Thank you.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You did not have one of those
last time you were here.

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:07.707 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I did not.
I do not take that lightly.

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:10.710
A lot of very brave sources
did some very tough stuff, so...

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.911 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And I want to talk
about the work

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:14.814
you did to win the Pulitzer,
but I also want to talk about

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:16.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
this book that is
separate from that.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.552 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I know -- We do a show
about breaking news.

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:21.755 align:start size:94% position:6%
we have to scramble
and cover things last minute.

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:23.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You do an incredible job.
-Well, thank you.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:27.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I love a fake
press briefing, right?

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:27.827 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-But you --

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.629
-You ask harder questions
than the actual press pool.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:32.032
-I know. Thank you very much.
Thanks for pointing that out.

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:33.867 align:start size:94% position:6%
But this is a book.
This is a book.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:37.103
And books have to be done
a lot longer in advance

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:39.172 line:92%
than something that I would do.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:40.807 line:92%
And you actually, in real time,

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.809 size:75% position:38%
sort of had to cover
the Tillerson firing

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
because this is about
Secretaries of State
and diplomacy.

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:47.814 line:92%
And did you interview Tillerson

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:50.283 align:start size:94% position:6%
after he was fired,
for the book, or before?

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:54.120 line:92%
-Tillerson, if he had any logic,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.123
knew that he was going to
be fired on some level.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:58.925 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe that's why
he's so frank in this book.

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.894
I mean, he basically says
"I didn't know what I was doing.

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:02.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wasn't experienced
enough for it."

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:05.865
Yeah, I set out to interview
every living Secretary of State.

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:07.934 size:84% position:42%
I got there in the end,
but the problem with

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:09.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
writing about
this administration

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:12.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
is people are fired
every five minutes.

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:12.672 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So it's, like,
rolling off the presses,

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I'm like, "Wait! Wait!
Stop the machines!

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:20.947 align:start size:94% position:6%
I need to add Mike Pompeo
to this book!"

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:23.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And you managed to.
-And I did.

00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:24.617 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-And that's interesting.

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:27.787 align:start size:94% position:6%
Again, it is very forthright
and frank,

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:29.689 line:92%
the way Tillerson talks about --

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:31.324 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's almost like
an exit interview

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:33.726 align:start size:94% position:6%
where he admits
he deserved to get fired.

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:34.394 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Were you surprised?

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:35.995 size:84% position:42%
-He lays a lot of blame
on this White House.

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:39.165 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was not surprised
because it is apparent

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:41.468 size:78% position:39%
to anyone looking at
the State Department,

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:42.469 line:92%
which I was for this book,

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:45.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it's become something
of a Dumpster fire,

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:46.539 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
which is sad

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:49.142
because there are brave men
and women serving there.

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:50.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
what I lay out in this book

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.312 line:92%
is it's unglamorous work.

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's not work that
people always understand

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:57.250
as much as what the military
does, but it really saves lives.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.852 line:92%
-And I do want to point out

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:01.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you do not lay
all of the blame

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:02.288 line:92%
on the feet of Republicans

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:04.390
insofar of the State Department
right now,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
that both Republicans
and Democrats have a problem

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:10.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
talking about the importance
of the diplomacy

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:11.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
vis-àà-vis the importance
of the military.

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:13.800 size:75% position:38%
-Yeah, so, you know,
these are the people

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
that, yes,
stamp passports and stuff

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:19.139
but also screen dangerous people
and prevent them from
coming into the country.

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:21.574
They are the people who save you
if you're kidnapped abroad.

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:24.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're the people who cut
the high-level political deals

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:28.281
that keep our servicemen and
women out of the line of fire.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.082 line:92%
They're really important.

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:32.385 align:start size:94% position:6%
But because, you know,
they're not on the front lines

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:33.686 line:92%
with guns making things go boom,

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:37.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
people on campaign trails
invariably kind of decry them

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
as dusty bureaucrats
who don't get anything done,

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:41.394
and that is true of both
Democrats And Republicans.

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:43.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
The Clinton administration
was terrible on this.

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:45.999 size:84% position:42%
They slashed and burned
spending on diplomacy.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:47.934 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was really bad for us
around the world.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is happening now
to a new extreme.

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, yeah,
this is about both parties.

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:53.640 size:88% position:44%
And a lot of Republicans
are in this book saying

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.241 size:84% position:42% line:92%
this is a huge problem.

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:55.909 size:78% position:39% line:92%
Colin Powell's saying

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:58.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're mortgaging our future
right now with this.

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:02.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You went and interviewed
an Afghani general.

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.083 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Is that Dostum?

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.251 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-General Dostum,

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:07.520 align:start size:94% position:6%
who is typically described
as a warlord,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
although in this book
he says he is a peace lord...

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.992 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-Oh, nice rebranding.

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
-...shortly before admitting
to dumping

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:18.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
hundreds, maybe thousands,
of Taliban prisoners

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:20.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
into a mass grave.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:22.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I don't know how that squares
with the peace-lord thing.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:28.474
-And what was your takeaway
insofar as how his rise to power

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:31.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
in Afghanistan reflects
on U.S. diplomacy?

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:32.712 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, one of the
interesting consequences

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:35.748
of kicking the diplomats and the
negotiators and the peacemakers

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:37.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
out of the room
is you end up with policy

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's made much more out of
the Pentagon and the CIA.

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:41.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there are
great people there,

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.489 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's an important part
of how we do business,

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:45.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
but when it's the only part
of how we do business,

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:47.293 line:92%
you end up with relationships

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:49.596 size:75% position:38%
like the one we have
with General Dostum.

00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:53.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
Which is we gave him
a ton of guns after 9/11.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:54.934 line:92%
He went after the Taliban.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
But then we're
kind of stuck with him.

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:58.771
And now he's the Vice President
of Afghanistan.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I chronicle how
multiple administrations

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:02.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
covered up for
his human-rights abuses,

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:05.278
including this mass grave,
which he kind of admits to,

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:06.579 line:92%
that his commanders did it.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:07.914 line:92%
And, you know, you meet him,

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:10.650
and it's pretty quickly apparent
that he's not necessarily

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.152 size:88% position:44%
the kind of guy you want
to be lying down with.

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:17.557 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yet when you met him,
he had a reindeer.

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:19.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He did have a reindeer.
-Yeah, okay.

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:22.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
You gonna explain
how an Afghani warlord

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:23.129 size:88% position:44% line:92%
ends up with a reindeer?

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:25.932
-So, I don't know if you can see
in this picture,

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:27.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
but there's, like,
kind of flashing

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:28.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
Christmas tree lights
everywhere.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:29.936 line:92%
There's tchotchkes around.

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:33.473
Like, porcelain figurines
of horseback fighters,

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:34.207 size:81% position:41% line:92%
which is what he does.

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:35.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
He has a sword,
and he's on horseback,

00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:36.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
like cutting heads off
and stuff.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:40.480
And, you know, this is like
his warlord sheik thing,

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.049 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I guess it fits that
he comes in with a reindeer,

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:45.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he sort of -- He gestures
to it like Vanna White

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:49.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
or something, like,
"Here's the reindeer."

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:51.791 size:81% position:41%
You know, there's guys
with guns behind him,

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:52.692 line:92%
so you don't want to offend him.

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:54.794 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it is --
It's a very nice reindeer.

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:57.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. And it's not
the reindeer's fault.

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:58.931 line:92%
I mean, he didn't choose the --

00:04:58.931 --> 00:05:01.234 align:start size:94% position:6%
But very sharp antlers
thrashing around.

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:03.870
So I'm, like, you know,
dodging the antlers and saying,

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Well, General,
what a lovely deer that is."

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
Do you think he was --

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:10.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because you were an American
he's like,

00:05:10.243 --> 00:05:12.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Oh, I know how
I'll put him at ease.

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:13.713 line:92%
I'll show him the reindeer."

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.548 line:92%
-Yeah. I think that's right.

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:16.949 align:start size:94% position:6%
It worked.
I felt very soothed.

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:19.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You were like,
"Oh. This is home."

